treeRowT -- DOES THE GENERATED TREE AGREE WITH THE INTERPRETIVE ONE? (SEQ 183-186, 2026-09-26)

THE QUESTION tree.sh and mixed.sh asked, carried onto the KANT road. Those two drove the
parseMethod= road, and nothing has reached a parseMethod=-bound body since 0150f29 (09-07), so
both compared the interpretive tree with ITSELF from then on. Ruling 1 retired that road.

THE SUBJECT is tree.sh's own section 2.4 case: an alternation whose winning option must arrive
RETAGGED under the enclosing rule's name -- ScafA or ScafI comes back as ScafALT. The literals
are LABELLED (lp-="(" rather than "("-) because a bare literal generates as GrouP(), and
generateParse refuses two of them in one rule.

THE TWO ARMS, one process. parser() is one-way, so the OLD arm runs first. Both read through
tell, i.e. through driveStep, and print through labelTree. Since the floor reader (SEQ 186) a
generated root's label is parked on the drive floor and handed back by driveStep; before it,
tell REFUSED a generated root outright.

H16, THE ANTI-VOID ROW: traceParse is on so the harness can prove which road each arm took --
the OLD arm's door reads hasNewParse=0, the NEW arm's hasNewParse=1. An earlier probe wrapped
ScafOUT in an old-road root to make tell work and printed identical trees on both arms, because
BOTH arms were interpretive. That is the failure this row exists to prevent.

THE READING, 2026-09-26: the trees AGREE -- ScafOUT over ScafALT for (a) and for (i), the
winner retagged on both roads; a reject hands back nothing on either. Pinned as agreement.
H7: exitFromParse built with promote=0 (no retag on the new road) turns the NEW tree to ScafA
and the row goes red.
